Answer: theia is a technique for localizing structural bugs in deep learning programs—such as issues with activation functions, layer/model properties, loss functions, data preprocessing, and missing, redundant, or wrong layers—and for suggesting fixes. it uses dataset characteristics and parsed source code, builds a library-independent meta-model, and applies verification rules with context-sensitive analyses to detect bugs in keras and pytorch models before training. it supports fcnns and cnns for regression and classification.

neuralint is an existing approach for localizing bugs in deep learning programs. it detects bugs before training and supports cnn architecture-related bugs; it was used as the comparison baseline for theia on 40 buggy deep learning programs.
